\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.1}{\ignorespaces Ingestion data flow: three source pipelines converging at shared JSONL files, then diverging into system-specific indexes. Chunking parameters (400-word sliding window, 60-word overlap) and the BGE-base-en-v1.5 embedding model are shared across both systems; the sole architectural difference is a single unified index for RAG versus three per-source indexes for the agent.}}{24}{figure.3.1}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.2}{\ignorespaces RAG pipeline query phase. The original query is passed to the LLM to generate a hypothetical passage (HyDE), whose embedding is used as the FAISS search vector. The original query is preserved for cross-encoder reranking, ensuring relevance scoring remains grounded in the actual question. MMR diversification ($\lambda = 0.7$) then selects the final five passages from the reranked pool before synthesis.}}{29}{figure.3.2}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.3}{\ignorespaces Single-agent ReAct loop. The language model alternates between Thought (reasoning) and Action (tool call) steps; each tool observation is appended to the growing context window and the cycle repeats until the model emits a final answer without a tool call, or the 15-iteration ceiling is reached.}}{33}{figure.3.3}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.4}{\ignorespaces Multi-agent coordination architecture. A single \texttt {retrieve\_all\_sources} node queries all three per-source FAISS indexes sequentially, accumulating five chunks per source. The \texttt {synthesize} node then issues exactly one LLM call over the pooled fifteen-chunk context. There is no reasoning loop and no iteration limit.}}{34}{figure.3.4}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.1}{\ignorespaces Answer relevancy (RAGAS) by system and query type. Error bars show 95\,\% bootstrap confidence intervals (percentile method, 10\,000 resamples; $n = 30$ factual/synthesis, $n = 15$ ambiguous). Intervals overlap substantially across the three systems without empty-answer failures; no significance testing was performed.}}{66}{figure.6.1}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.2}{\ignorespaces Mean latency by query type and system. The single agent's unanswerable latency of 22.9\,s — fifteen times the original RAG baseline — is the most pronounced divergence across all systems and query types, resulting from exhaustive search up to the iteration ceiling on all 15 unanswerable queries. Synthesis queries are the slowest category for every system except the single agent.}}{69}{figure.6.2}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6.3}{\ignorespaces Complexity ratings by system and criterion. Higher ratings indicate lower implementation, operational, or maintenance complexity. The author assigned the ratings using the rubric in Section~\ref {subsec:rubric}. Quantitative proxies, including lines of code and source-onboarding step counts, informed the ratings where available; the remaining ratings reflect the author's documented implementation observations. As these are single-evaluator assessments, they should be interpreted descriptively.}}{71}{figure.6.3}%
